ECHO(1)                        P32W Unix                         ECHO(1)

NAME
       echo - print the arguments

SYNOPSIS
       echo [-n] [arg ...]

DESCRIPTION
       echo writes its arguments to the standard output, separated
       by single blanks and followed by a newline.

OPTIONS
       -n     no newline at the end. Only the first argument is
              taken as an option.

EXAMPLES
       echo hello, world
       echo PST8PDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0 > /etc/TZ
       echo -n 'continue? '

EXIT STATUS
       0.

SEE ALSO
       sh(1), cat(1)

BUGS
       Backslash escapes (\n, \c, \t) are not interpreted; they are
       printed as typed. -- is not special and is printed.
